WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.180
- You're a nurturer.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.110
You've taken care of
people your whole life.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.470
- Yes, three days a week anyway

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.270
'cause I'll work out on 14th street.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.380
So you and I, we're just
gonna make this a thing.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.790
- Well, and you're a nurturer.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.110
You've taken care

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.249
of people your whole life.
- Yes, ma'am.

00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:16.540
Are we crying today?

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.290
- We might, it happens here
on this show 'cause it's life.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.870
And we don't try to pretend
it's anything, but what it is.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:27.270
But I just really admire your journey

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.000
and I just wanna be a part of your crew.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.250
- Oh, thank you so much.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.260
Well, I'm gonna give you
guys a little cliff notes

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.650
of Millie Peartree really quickly.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.440
So at the age of 25, I lost
my mom to colon cancer.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.640
And I had to take care of my
four brothers and sisters,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.940
two of them are autistic.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.180
So my life has been full of ups and downs,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.800
but I say it's most important
that you do the pivot

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.030
like we all get sad, we lose people.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.430
I'm pretty sure throughout
the last two years,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.700
everybody has been affected

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.260
by something, somehow, some way

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.340
whether they knew somebody
that lost someone,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.090
whether they lost somebody personally,

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.920
but it's all about,
you gotta pick self up,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.950
you gotta continue to go.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.350
So even in 2019,

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.170
I lost my restaurant
right before the pandemic.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.020
Illegal gasoline work
done in the building,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.980
so I lost my business and it was like,

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.080
you just gotta keep going.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.580
So when the pandemic happened,
I caught COVID early on

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.160
and this was during the
time of like no masks.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.940
We really didn't know what was going on.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.121
- [Drew] I did too.

00:01:23.121 --> 00:01:24.600
- We just knew when you lost a sense

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.210
of taste and smell, that
wasn't a good thing.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.650
- [Drew] Oh God.
- But as I got better,

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.600
when it started to open up a little bit,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.240
we started to feed essential workers.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.831
And essential workers, it was just like,

00:01:34.831 --> 00:01:36.690
we wanted to be able to help people

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.980
and feed people because
everything was closed.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.740
- And that is the caretaker in you.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.020
- Yes, ma'am, yes, ma'am.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.650
- It is, you're such a good person.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.160
And then you get a call from Chip and Joe.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.440
- Yeah, so I was doing the work.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.260
We fed over 100,000 children

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:52.570
and families throughout the pandemic.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.403
- [Drew] Oh my God.

00:01:53.403 --> 00:01:55.797
I'm just sorry, we just
gotta get the hands for that.

00:01:55.797 --> 00:01:56.902
(audience clap)

00:01:56.902 --> 00:01:59.266
- And then I get this message.

00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:01.040
I started to cook, and bake,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.370
and just try to be
happier and help people.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.000
And I get this call from Chip and Joanna.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.537
And they're like, "You know what?

00:02:07.537 --> 00:02:09.487
"We're just inspired by everything you do.

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:10.860
"Let's do something."

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.780
And I was like, "Okay,
well let's do something."

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.610
And that's how "A chef's guide
to soul food" came about.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.690
- I love this story so much.
(audience clap)

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.680
Now Millie, one of the things
we're gonna tackle today

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.680
is something that I think so
many people are in the dilemma

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.260
of is leftovers.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.750
And I heard you say you
can and will reinvent them

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.750
so there is no food waste and
I love this particular recipe.

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.820
And I'm like, teach us.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.840
I'm in your car.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.390
You are the driver, teach us
Millie, what are we doing?

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.650
- Absolutely, so people
oftentimes say, you know what,

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.590
I don't eat leftovers.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.350
I'm a big leftover girl.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.280
I just feel like it tastes
better the next day.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.080
But some people, they
just don't care for it.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.070
But we live in a society

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.740
where we shouldn't be
throwing away food, right?

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.573
- [Drew] I agree.

00:03:00.573 --> 00:03:03.593
- So this recipe is my
Magnolia macaroni and cheese.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.070
- [Drew] You mean this right here?

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:07.010
- [Millie] Yes, I want you to taste that

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.480
because I heard that
that's your favorite thing

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.700
is macaroni and cheese.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.143
- It's my number one food on planet earth.

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:16.945
I'm salivating.
- Yep, just go in for it.

00:03:20.095 --> 00:03:22.055
See, see, there you go, that's right.

00:03:22.055 --> 00:03:23.928
Yep, that's what we're looking for.

00:03:23.928 --> 00:03:27.300
(audience cheer and clap)

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.350
- Millie, keep going.

00:03:29.622 --> 00:03:30.455
- So what we're gonna do

00:03:30.455 --> 00:03:31.430
is we're gonna take
this macaroni and cheese

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.090
and we're gonna repurpose
it into something else.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.330
So we're gonna make a
macaroni and cheese waffle.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.530
You get that crispy crunchy
with a little bit more cheese.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.610
So you get that ooey, gooey moment of it.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.330
But then oftentimes we
have leftover protein.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.130
So sometimes people cook
protein, they may overcook it

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.620
and maybe dried out.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.070
So in this pan, we have a
tablespoon or so of olive oil.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.860
We have some caramelized onions.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.290
And then we're gonna add some garlic.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.470
I love garlic.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.000
This is about six cloves,
but add more or less

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.240
if you like.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.260
- We're also gonna bring some
brisket back to life too.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.560
- We're gonna bring some
brisket back to life.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.110
So we already have some
chopped brisket here,

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.593
and we're gonna dump this in.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.500
Add that, and it smells good already.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.280
And because I love soul
food and Southern food

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.050
because soul food and
Southern food are different.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.020
They're seven cuisine to Southern food,

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.120
but we're gonna take a Cajun seasoning.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.940
We're gonna add a little
bit more spice to it.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:22.910
- How much Millie?

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:23.960
- As much as you like.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.389
I do a big pinch.

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:27.533
- I'm not subtle about anything.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.050
- And when I make my Cajun
seasoning, I do it salt free

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.120
to your point is you're
not subtle about it

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.110
so it gives you all that flavor,

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:36.610
but you're not making it
salty, 'cause don't forget,

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.210
we already cooked this already
and it has salt in the meat.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.410
And then so we have this going

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.910
and then we're gonna add some stock.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:44.610
- Yeah, 'cause brisket can get dry.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.040
- It can get dry and we're
just re-hydrating it.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.370
- [Drew] Is this stock?

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:49.203
- This is stock.

00:04:49.203 --> 00:04:50.510
This is beef stock we're using.

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.050
We're adding flavor upon flavor.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.050
If you have chicken stock,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.380
if you have like some beef
bullion, whatever it is,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.490
you have vegetable stock
or you can even add water.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.330
- See, this is what I
mean about reconstituting.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.350
You are bringing this into
not just back to life,

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.183
but a new life.

00:05:04.183 --> 00:05:06.730
- Absolutely, and it'll
be equally delicious.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.350
And then we've already added some stock

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.950
and now we're gonna add
some barbecue sauce.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.596
- [Drew] Oh, okay.

00:05:12.596 --> 00:05:14.260
- [Audience Member] Mmh!

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.310
- I heard somebody saying, "Mmh"

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.300
- I mean, God, I love barbecue sauce.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.420
- I love barbecue sauce.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.970
And if you make barbecue sauce,

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.960
feel free to use a jarred
barbecue sauce if you like.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.540
It's all about making things your own.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.130
And that's one thing I like
about soul food so much.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:31.690
You make things your own.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.140
And I show you guys how I make cornbread,

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.900
and macaroni cheese, and
everything in my Magnolia project.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.070
I put cinnamon in my cornbread.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.790
But if you don't like
cinnamon, don't use cinnamon.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.360
It's all about showing you the technique

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.840
on the reason why I do things.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.010
And it's most important
that we're telling a story.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.410
And that's the one thing I like
about the Magnolia Network.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.360
They got us a whole bunch

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.440
of us storytellers together and we're able

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.410
to tell stories.
- Yes, absolutely.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.763
Now, how long do I wanna
cook the liquid out?

00:05:57.763 --> 00:05:59.180
(audience clap)

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:03.010
How long would you suggest
that I let this go Millie?

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.680
- So we'll let this go for
about 10 or 15 minutes.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:05.560
You're gonna constantly stir it.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.030
- Oh see, I'm glad I asked.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.910
I would've pulled this off and
had something kind of drippy

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.240
and I would not have
thought to leave it in there

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.937
for 10 or 15 minutes and
I'm really glad I asked.

00:06:14.937 --> 00:06:15.770
- 10 to 15 minutes
because re-hydrating it.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:16.890
If you overcook it,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.780
and then it can pull
together a little bit more

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.510
so you can have like this pulled brisket.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:21.400
But most importantly,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.410
you wanna rehydrate it.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.580
You want the flavor, and you
want it to be very moist.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.347
- [Drew] See, this is why I
don't know what I'm doing.

00:06:26.347 --> 00:06:28.930
(upbeat music)

